## 📄 STAGE 2: Direct PDF Ingestion & 60 FPS Viewport Architecture

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor QE as Quality Engineer
    participant WebApp as Next.js WebApp UI
    participant Gateway as Node.js API Gateway
    participant Redis as Redis Cache
    participant S3 as AWS S3 Storage Bucket
    participant Engine as WebGL / Canvas Viewport

    QE->>WebApp: 1. Drag & Drop PDF Blueprint (e.g. Turbine_Blade_RevC.pdf)
    WebApp->>Gateway: 2. Request Presigned Upload URL (drawing_id, file_size)
    Gateway->>Redis: 3. Verify User JWT & Tenant Storage Quota
    Gateway-->>WebApp: 4. Return S3 Presigned PUT URL (15-min expiration)
    WebApp->>S3: 5. Direct Binary Upload PDF to S3 (Bypasses API Servers)
    S3-->>WebApp: 6. Upload Complete 200 OK
    WebApp->>Engine: 7. Initialize WebGL Viewport Engine (PDF.js Vector Core)
    Engine-->>QE: 8. Render Blueprint at 60 FPS (Pan, Zoom, Rotate, Sheets, Layers)
```

### Stage 2 Execution Flow:
1. **Presigned Direct Upload:** The browser requests a short-lived S3 presigned PUT URL and uploads the blueprint PDF directly to **AWS S3**, completely avoiding backend server bottlenecks.
2. **Desktop-Grade Viewport:** The client initializes a **WebGL / Canvas rendering engine** capable of rendering multi-page vector drawings up to $3200\%$ magnification at 60 FPS with sheet navigation and layer toggling.

